// This strategy tracks the usual constrained-decoding state
// (generated/current constrained span) plus one task-specific signal:
// whether the active arithmetic span appears to be in a "narrow" position
// where caller-supplied token groups should be boosted. The intended task is
// step-by-step math reasoning with every arithmetic computation wrapped in
// << >>, so outside spans we generate freely until the model emits "<<";
// inside spans we keep the content parser-valid and favor tokens from
// validTokenGroups when the parser's valid-next set is small enough that a
// gentle bias is unlikely to distort fluent generation.
//
// Action choice is based on three observations each iteration:
// (1) outside a span, unconstrained text is best generated freely;
// (2) once inside a span, if the parser says the span is complete we close it
// immediately with ">>";
// (3) otherwise we either use AdaptiveConstrainedStep with group boosts at
// narrow positions or plain ConstrainedStep when the grammar is wider.
//
// The tracked state supports valid generation because currentConstrainedOut
// always mirrors exactly the suffix of generated that lies between the most
// recent "<<" and the eventual ">>", and every inside-span token is obtained
// from helpers that guarantee parser-valid continuation (or EOS, which stops).
// CSD_RATIONALE_END
// CSD_PROOF_SKETCH_BEGIN
// parser_validity:
// - Unconstrained branch: if EOS we break with state unchanged; otherwise we
//   append one token to generated, and only when that token is "<<" do we set
//   insideConstrainedOut := true with currentConstrainedOut := [], which is a
//   valid prefix by precondition.
// - Close branch: CloseConstrainedSpan sets insideConstrainedOut to false and
//   currentConstrainedOut to [], so the implication is vacuous afterward.
// - Adaptive constrained branch: AdaptiveConstrainedStep returns either EOS or
//   a token next such that currentConstrainedOut + [next] is parser-valid;
//   AppendConstrainedToken therefore preserves parser validity.
// - Plain constrained branch: ConstrainedStep returns unchanged state on EOS,
//   or parser-valid updated constrained state directly on non-EOS.
//
// suffix:
// - Unconstrained branch: when we stay outside, the implication is vacuous;
//   when we open a span on token "<<", we set currentConstrainedOut := [], and
//   the length-0 suffix of generated is [].
// - Close branch: CloseConstrainedSpan appends at most the closing delimiter
//   and resets currentConstrainedOut to [], so afterward insideConstrainedOut
//   is false and the implication is vacuous.
// - Adaptive constrained branch: on non-EOS we append the same next token to
//   both generated and currentConstrainedOut via AppendConstrainedToken, so the
//   suffix equality is preserved; on EOS we break with unchanged state.
// - Plain constrained branch: ConstrainedStep's postcondition preserves the
//   suffix invariant internally on non-EOS, and leaves state unchanged on EOS.
//
// cost accounting:
// - We return cost := steps, so the loop only needs to maintain steps <=
//   maxSteps. Every non-breaking branch increments steps by exactly 1 after a
//   cost-bumping helper call (UnconstrainedStep, CloseConstrainedSpan,
//   AdaptiveConstrainedStep, or ConstrainedStep).
// - Breaking branches occur only immediately after such a helper consumed one
//   step, so the final assignment cost := steps satisfies cost <= maxSteps.
//
// progress:
// - Unconstrained branch appends exactly one token and then does steps :=
//   steps + 1, so |generated| grows by at most 1 while the bound grows by 1.
// - Close branch appends at most one token and increments steps by 1, so the
//   bound is preserved.
// - Adaptive constrained branch appends exactly one token only in the non-EOS
//   case via AppendConstrainedToken, and still increments steps by 1; the EOS
//   case breaks immediately.
// - Plain constrained branch either leaves generated unchanged on EOS and
//   breaks, or ConstrainedStep appends one token and we increment steps by 1.
//   Thus every non-breaking branch strictly increases steps, justifying
//   decreases maxSteps - steps.
// CSD_PROOF_SKETCH_END
